{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"paradoxal-unquenched-snowmonkey","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Four smaller defects found by the same sandbox validation that produced tickets\n`grapy-agronomic-sealion` (device-resolution guessing) and `unamused-cloudy-junebug` (missing\n/var/gerrit mount assertion). Grouped because each is small and they share a file.\n\n## F3 (MEDIUM) - fstab entries accumulate on volume replacement\n\n`mount_ebs_volume` guards with `grep -q \"$uuid\" /etc/fstab`, which only checks for the NEW UUID.\nNothing removes a previous volume's line for the SAME mount point. The runbook's own\n\"restore onto a fresh volume\" path triggers it. PROVEN:\n\n```\n### T8  STALE fstab entry after a volume REPLACEMENT\n   old UUID=c814b8a1-...  new UUID=b1e23678-...\n     UUID=c814b8a1-dba9-4a59-b84c-0a6d6b6b38b2 /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch xfs defaults,nofail 0 2\n     UUID=b1e23678-982c-4a83-b6ab-d5d144c1f57f /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch xfs defaults,nofail 0 2\n  [DEFECT]  2 fstab entries for the SAME mount point; the stale one is a dangling nofail entry\n            that persists across reboots\n```\n\n`nofail` HIDES it, so which volume ends up at the mount point becomes fstab-order-dependent —\npermanently, and silently. That is a latent wrong-data-at-the-mount-point hazard, not merely\nuntidy.\n\n- [ ] AC1 - Writing an fstab entry for a mount point REMOVES any pre-existing entry for that same\n      mount point that is not the current UUID. Prove with the replacement scenario above:\n      exactly one entry afterwards.\n- [ ] AC2 - The prior fstab is backed up before modification, so a bad edit is recoverable.\n\n## F4 (LOW) - chmod runs AFTER the marker writes\n\nScript order is `touch` declaration -> `touch` proof -> `chmod 0700` (`:129-131`). The recorded\nintent (and the runbook) place `chmod` BEFORE the markers. Impact is small — root-owned, brief\n0755 window — but script and documented intent diverge, and the divergence is the kind that\nquietly becomes load-bearing.\n\n- [ ] AC3 - `chmod 0700` precedes the marker writes, matching the documented sequence; or the\n      documented sequence is corrected to match the script, with the reason recorded. One of the\n      two, not both left disagreeing.\n\n## F5 (LOW) - the two resolution branches return different string forms\n\n```\n   nvme-cli path returns the GLOB path: /dev/nvme2n1\n   by-id fallback returns the REALPATH: /dev/loop2\n```\nSame device, different string. Harmless today; it means log lines differ by which branch fired,\nand any future device COMPARISON would be branch-dependent.\n\n- [ ] AC4 - Both branches return the same canonical form for the same device.\n\n## F6 (LOW) - `gate_scratch_mount` is not actually single-sourced\n\n`infra/terraform/variables.tf:63` states that four things must agree and that the variable is\nwhere the value is single-sourced. But `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:268` HARDCODES\n`REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR: \"/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch\"` rather than deriving it. Changing the terraform\ndefault would desync them — producing exactly the silent fallback the variable's own documentation\nwarns about.\n\n- [ ] AC5 - Either the compose value is derived from the single source, or `variables.tf:63`'s\n      claim is corrected to name the places that must be updated together. The current state,\n      where the doc asserts single-sourcing that does not exist, is the defect.\n\n## Validation provenance\n\nAll findings executed in a real Ubuntu 24.04 aarch64 VM: real `losetup` loop devices, real\n`mkfs.xfs` (xfsprogs 6.6.0), real `mount -a`, real `blkid`/`mountpoint`, rendered template run\nverbatim under `unshare --mount --propagation private`. Only `nvme-cli` was stubbed, since loop\ndevices have no NVMe identify page. `shellcheck -s bash` on the rendered script is clean.","id":"ad8d-4274-ef43-4f44","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"user_data.sh hardening: fstab entries accumulate on volume replacement, chmod/marker ordering diverges from intent, resolution branches return different device forms, and gate_scratch_mount is not actually single-sourced"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788550902479130001,"uuid":"415be0af-e764-47c6-8aca-6843e2235554"}